# M28 — app.pgrst_schema_exposed() weakened from exact membership to a substring test.
#
# THIS IS THE ONLY MUTATION IN THE SET THAT MAKES THE CODE LOOK BETTER. Every other entry
# removes or neuters a guard in a way a reviewer would question; this one replaces a
# string_to_array / unnest / btrim scan with a single LIKE and reads as a simplification.
# It is also WRONG, in a direction that matters: a list naming `application` or `app_private`
# would satisfy a query for `app`, so the rule would report the REST path healthy on a
# project where schema `app` is not exposed at all and every submit answers PGRST106 — the
# precise defect that shipped.
#
# It passes nine of the eleven cases in the membership test. The two it fails are the two
# that exist for it. That asymmetry is the point: a case list without the substring traps
# would accept this mutation and the test would look thorough while proving the weaker
# property.
#
# This function is called from THREE places — rule E1, app.pgrst_schema_list_with(), and the
# migration's own 1b block — so a single definition is also what stops those three from
# drifting (canon 4.4's argument for one derivation function serving both the generated
# column and the trigger).
id: M28
title: app.pgrst_schema_exposed() weakened from trimmed exact membership to a substring match
expect: caught
proves: the membership test discriminates `app` from `application` and `app_private`, so rule E1 cannot be satisfied by a schema that merely shares a prefix
expect-test: [NFR-01] decides membership by trimmed exact match, never by substring

file: supabase/migrations/20261022160000_expose_app_schema_to_postgrest.sql
count: 1
@@FIND
      where btrim(s) = btrim(p_schema)
@@REPLACE
      where coalesce(p_recorded, '') like '%' || btrim(p_schema) || '%'
@@END
